1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,439
welcome to this new analysis.

2
00:00:02,439 --> 00:00:07,879
Today, we're taking an unfiltered look at one of the most fascinating paradoxes of contemporary economics.

3
00:00:07,879 --> 00:00:13,640
Is democracy truly an engine for economic growth, or is it a bureaucratic hindrance?

4
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:18,440
We often hear that governments with an iron fist can impose necessary reforms

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:21,440
much faster, but what do the real data tell us?

6
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,679
Those measured in the field and free from ideologies.

7
00:00:24,679 --> 00:00:29,160
Well, let's set aside personal opinions and dive into the facts.

8
00:00:29,199 --> 00:00:30,960
Here's today's agenda.

9
00:00:30,960 --> 00:00:33,480
One, the debate on democracy.

10
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:36,520
Two, the optical illusion of historical data.

11
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:39,200
Three, the new methodological lens.

12
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:41,679
Four, the real effects on GDP.

13
00:00:41,679 --> 00:00:43,920
Five, the drivers of growth.

14
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,840
And finally, six, institutions for prosperity.

15
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:49,200
Let's start right away.

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,760
First point, the debate on democracy.

17
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:56,480
In recent decades, a somewhat pessimistic idea has spread.

18
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,200
There's this underlying skepticism.

19
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:01,960
Very well summarized by a bold phrase from economist Robert Barro,

20
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:06,480
who says, greater political rights have no effect on growth.

21
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:10,680
In short, the myth is that an authoritarian regime can just push straight ahead

22
00:01:10,680 --> 00:01:13,040
and make the economy grow much faster,

23
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:16,840
completely bypassing lengthy parliamentary discussions.

24
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,800
And here we move on to point two.

25
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:21,359
The optical illusion of data.

26
00:01:21,359 --> 00:01:24,000
Now, if we look at certain historical data,

27
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,200
it would almost seem that this skepticism is justified.

28
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:29,519
But there's a trick.

29
00:01:29,519 --> 00:01:32,519
There's literally an optical illusion at play.

30
00:01:32,519 --> 00:01:37,680
Observing the years leading up to when a country becomes democratic,

31
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:40,799
one always notices a dramatic collapse in wealth,

32
00:01:40,799 --> 00:01:44,799
a real economic slump just before the regime change.

33
00:01:44,799 --> 00:01:48,400
But pay attention, because this is the core of the whole issue.

34
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:51,920
It is absolutely not the arrival of democracy that causes that collapse,

35
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:55,439
it's the exact opposite, it's deep economic crises,

36
00:01:55,439 --> 00:01:57,120
that trigger democracy.

37
00:01:57,120 --> 00:02:00,320
Autocracy fails, poverty skyrockets,

38
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:05,000
and at that point, the population takes to the streets to demand political rights.

39
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,840
Confusing the cause with the effect,

40
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:11,000
has unfortunately fueled decades of completely unjustified pessimism.

41
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:15,920
This brings us directly to point three, the new methodological lens.

42
00:02:15,920 --> 00:02:21,719
To dismantle this illusion, researchers had to radically change their approach.

43
00:02:21,719 --> 00:02:25,840
The old method simply compared countries at a given moment,

44
00:02:25,840 --> 00:02:29,479
falling completely into the trap of the optical illusion.

45
00:02:29,479 --> 00:02:33,319
The new method, however, uses an extremely advanced strategy,

46
00:02:33,319 --> 00:02:37,079
which takes into account all the unique characteristics of a nation,

47
00:02:37,079 --> 00:02:41,199
and especially the dynamic past history of its GDP.

48
00:02:41,199 --> 00:02:44,520
To put it more simply, this dynamic panel strategy

49
00:02:44,520 --> 00:02:47,599
acts as a powerful purification filter,

50
00:02:47,599 --> 00:02:51,879
mathematically subtracting the damage and noise of the pre-existing crisis.

51
00:02:51,879 --> 00:02:56,280
By cleaning the data in this way, we can finally see the raw and pure reality,

52
00:02:56,280 --> 00:02:58,439
what really happens to the economy,

53
00:02:58,439 --> 00:03:01,519
when democratic institutions take solid root.

54
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:05,560
And so, point 4, the true effects on GDP.

55
00:03:05,560 --> 00:03:11,159
Drum roll, 20%, exactly, a solid 20%.

56
00:03:11,159 --> 00:03:14,039
Global historical data, once purified,

57
00:03:14,039 --> 00:03:18,479
clearly tell us that transitioning to a democracy, permanently,

58
00:03:18,479 --> 00:03:22,519
increases per capita GDP by 20% in the long run,

59
00:03:22,519 --> 00:03:26,560
compared to the scenario where that country had remained a dictatorship,

60
00:03:26,560 --> 00:03:29,799
an absolutely undeniable leap forward.

61
00:03:29,799 --> 00:03:33,639
And pay attention, it's not magic that happens overnight,

62
00:03:33,639 --> 00:03:35,959
wealth is built over time,

63
00:03:35,959 --> 00:03:39,039
looking at the trajectory, one sees that after the transition,

64
00:03:39,039 --> 00:03:42,039
wealth rises steadily, year after year,

65
00:03:42,039 --> 00:03:46,560
then stabilizing at its peak after about 20 or 25 years.

66
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:49,840
In short, democracy is a structural investment

67
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:53,799
that needs its time, but ultimately pays off enormously.

68
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:57,799
We come to point 5, the drivers of growth.

69
00:03:57,799 --> 00:04:01,560
It spontaneously arises to ask how, in practice, a change

70
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:05,359
in political rights transforms into real money for the economy.

71
00:04:05,359 --> 00:04:07,239
Well, the data speaks for itself.

72
00:04:07,239 --> 00:04:09,319
Democracies do one fundamental thing

73
00:04:09,319 --> 00:04:11,319
that autocracies often ignore,

74
00:04:11,319 --> 00:04:14,560
they invest in people in broad-spectrum public goods.

75
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:16,560
We're talking about massive capital investments,

76
00:04:16,560 --> 00:04:18,680
a boom in high school enrollments,

77
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:20,879
huge and widespread improvements in healthcare,

78
00:04:20,879 --> 00:04:24,639
and, crucially, a drastic reduction in social unrest.

79
00:04:24,639 --> 00:04:28,120
It is this solid mixture that generates that extra 20%.

80
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:32,079
Finally, point 6, institutions for prosperity.

81
00:04:32,079 --> 00:04:34,600
There's one last false myth to debunk.

82
00:04:34,600 --> 00:04:38,039
There's this widespread belief that a country

83
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:42,240
must already be rich to afford democracy.

84
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,360
Completely false.

85
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:47,760
The analysis shows us that the positive effect on growth

86
00:04:47,760 --> 00:04:51,720
does not depend at all on how developed a country is to begin with,

87
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:53,560
whether one starts from conditions of poverty

88
00:04:53,560 --> 00:04:55,720
or is already developing,

89
00:04:55,720 --> 00:04:57,720
embracing political freedom

90
00:04:57,720 --> 00:05:00,600
always brings a concrete material benefit.

91
00:05:00,600 --> 00:05:04,560
We conclude this analysis with a question rigorously based

92
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:07,439
on decades of objective measurements.

93
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,319
If the data so clearly demonstrate

94
00:05:10,319 --> 00:05:13,040
that political rights alone generate,

95
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:16,639
a 20% increase in long-term wealth,

96
00:05:16,639 --> 00:05:19,800
does it still make sense today to separate political freedom

97
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:21,439
from material well-being,

98
00:05:21,439 --> 00:05:23,800
it's a consideration that leaves its mark.

99
00:05:23,800 --> 00:05:26,800
The right to vote is not only a great moral achievement,

100
00:05:26,800 --> 00:05:30,800
ultimately, it is the first true structural engine of the economy.